WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:13.920
Fogg and his eighty-day adventure were soon overshadowed by other events.

2
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:22.522
A report appeared describing the "gentleman" robber who had stolen the fifty-five-thousand pounds, and the newspapers were caught

3
00:00:22.562 --> 00:00:24.843
up once again with the details of the robbery.

4
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:28.784
The police were looking for suspects all over Europe.

5
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.722
One agent in the vast manhunt was a short, slightly built man with a nervous, intelligent face and

6
00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:36.363
bright eyes.

7
00:00:37.943 --> 00:00:42.605
Detective Fix had been sent to Suez to watch every person who arrived there.

8
00:00:43.846 --> 00:00:49.828
His instructions were to investigate any suspicious characters and certainly anyone who resembled the thief.

9
00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:53.830
Fix stood on the dock, looking out at the water.

10
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.932
His eyebrows twitched nonstop, which was always a sign of his impatience.

11
00:01:00.288 --> 00:01:03.051
"Excuse me," Fix called to a passing sailor.

12
00:01:03.071 --> 00:01:07.294
"Can you tell me if the Mongolia is expected on time?"

13
00:01:07.314 --> 00:01:12.058
"It's a very punctual ship, it is," replied the sailor.

14
00:01:12.078 --> 00:01:14.801
"It has won awards for its speed."

15
00:01:16.282 --> 00:01:19.004
Fix felt that he had a good sense for spotting criminals.

16
00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:22.467
He had arrested plenty in his time.

17
00:01:23.788 --> 00:01:27.412
If the thief were on board the Mongolia, he wouldn't sneak past Fix.

18
00:01:29.836 --> 00:01:32.819
"What a magnificent robbery it was," Fix thought.

19
00:01:32.839 --> 00:01:35.881
"Fifty-five-thousand pounds!

20
00:01:36.702 --> 00:01:39.484
And by a man who resembles an honest gentleman.

21
00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:40.946
It's extraordinary!

22
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.669
Thieves are getting to be bold nowadays.

23
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:50.954
But then those fellows never have rascally faces, or they would be arrested immediately.

24
00:01:50.974 --> 00:01:57.100
No, I'm sure this thief looks honest, and I'm going to find him."

25
00:01:58.644 --> 00:02:03.485
The Mongolia was stopping in Suez for only a few hours to refuel, so Fix did not have

26
00:02:03.525 --> 00:02:05.046
a lot of time to spot his man.

27
00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:13.108
Fix took his position, and when the ship arrived and the passengers disembarked, he carefully examined every face

28
00:02:13.148 --> 00:02:13.708
that passed.

29
00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:19.470
One of the passengers vigorously pushed his way through the crowd and came right up to Fix.

30
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:26.192
The stranger held out a passport, explaining that he wished to have it stamped, and asked directions to

31
00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.112
the British consulate.

32
00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:31.970
Fix instinctively took the passport and read the description of its owner.

33
00:02:33.471 --> 00:02:35.052
His eyebrows lifted in surprise.

34
00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:37.834
The description perfectly matched the bank thief.

35
00:02:37.854 --> 00:02:40.656
"Is this your passport?"

36
00:02:41.096 --> 00:02:41.797
asked Fix.

37
00:02:43.018 --> 00:02:47.681
The stranger was Passepartout, and the passport in his hand belonged to Phileas Fogg.

38
00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:51.684
"No, it's my master's," replied Passepartout.

39
00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.025
"And where is your master now?"

40
00:02:54.485 --> 00:02:55.166
asked Fix.

41
00:02:55.186 --> 00:02:58.948
"He stayed onboard," said Passepartout.

42
00:02:58.968 --> 00:03:01.889
"He must go to the consul's office in person," said Fix.

43
00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:04.310
"And where is the consulate?

44
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.411
My master won't be pleased to be disturbed," replied Passepartout, frowning.

45
00:03:08.431 --> 00:03:10.671
"The consulate is not far.

46
00:03:11.732 --> 00:03:14.552
Have you or your master been to Suez before?"

47
00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:18.653
asked Fix, hoping for more information about the owner of the passport.

48
00:03:18.673 --> 00:03:21.834
"I have not," said Passepartout.

49
00:03:21.854 --> 00:03:24.335
"And I do not expect to see much of it.

50
00:03:25.335 --> 00:03:28.256
In fact, I expected my master to stop in Paris to see the sights.

51
00:03:29.356 --> 00:03:33.198
But all I saw of that city was seen as we rushed from one train station to another."

52
00:03:34.338 --> 00:03:35.979
"You are in a great hurry then?"

53
00:03:36.559 --> 00:03:38.460
asked Fix, growing more curious.

54
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.902
"I am not, but my master is," said Passepartout.

55
00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:45.803
"By the way, I must buy some shirts and shoes.

56
00:03:46.483 --> 00:03:49.044
We were in such a rush, we left with only a carpetbag."

57
00:03:49.064 --> 00:03:52.565
"But where is your master going?"

58
00:03:53.146 --> 00:03:53.826
asked Fix.

59
00:03:53.846 --> 00:03:59.568
"He is going around the world in eighty days," said Passepartout.

60
00:03:59.588 --> 00:04:02.731
"He says it's a wager, but between you and me, I don't believe a word of it!

61
00:04:04.112 --> 00:04:05.673
I think he has another reason for the trip."

62
00:04:05.693 --> 00:04:08.275
"Ah! Mr.

63
00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:10.097
Fogg is a character, is he?

64
00:04:11.058 --> 00:04:11.578
Is he rich?"

65
00:04:12.058 --> 00:04:13.800
asked Fix as his eyes lit up.

66
00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.663
"No doubt," replied Passepartout.

67
00:04:18.343 --> 00:04:22.167
"He is carrying an enormous sum in brand-new banknotes with him."

68
00:04:22.187 --> 00:04:25.669
"And have you known your master long?"

69
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.472
asked Fix, his voice filled with excitement.

70
00:04:29.888 --> 00:04:32.730
"Why, no, I entered his service the same day we left London.

71
00:04:34.211 --> 00:04:37.473
I will fetch him now and bring him to the consulate," Passepartout said.

72
00:04:39.835 --> 00:04:42.637
Passepartout's words had deeply intrigued the detective.

73
00:04:44.238 --> 00:04:48.221
Mr. Fogg had departed hastily from London, quite soon after the robbery.

74
00:04:48.681 --> 00:04:53.885
And the large sum of bank notes he carried was unusual, as was his peculiar eagerness to reach

75
00:04:53.945 --> 00:04:54.806
distant countries.

76
00:04:55.646 --> 00:04:57.608
All of it fit Fix's theory of the robber.

77
00:04:59.712 --> 00:05:01.993
Fix rapidly made his way to the British consulate.

78
00:05:04.134 --> 00:05:07.976
He was sure there would be a large reward for capturing such a bold criminal.

79
00:05:10.217 --> 00:05:16.359
The detective burst into the consul's office and exclaimed, "Consul, there's a thief on board the Mongolia.

80
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.760
He will be here any moment.

81
00:05:19.761 --> 00:05:21.081
You must detain him!"

82
00:05:25.223 --> 00:05:26.524
The race against time was on!

83
00:05:27.488 --> 00:05:31.752
We still had a long way to go, but luckily the Suez Canal had opened in 1869.

84
00:05:33.173 --> 00:05:37.096
The Suez Canal was the fastest way to go from Europe to India and Asia.

85
00:05:38.957 --> 00:05:42.821
Before it was built, goods had to be unloaded from ships and carried overland.

86
00:05:44.202 --> 00:05:48.385
Otherwise ships had to sail around all of Africa, and that was a rough and dangerous journey.

87
00:05:50.507 --> 00:05:51.668
I'm glad we didn't go that way!

88
00:05:52.896 --> 00:05:57.359
The Suez Canal is located in Egypt and links the Mediterranean Sea to the Red Sea.

89
00:05:58.999 --> 00:06:03.142
People think the ancient Egyptians first built a canal there nearly four-thousand years ago.

90
00:06:04.582 --> 00:06:07.924
Since then the canal has been destroyed, rebuilt, and destroyed again.

91
00:06:09.765 --> 00:06:14.428
Finally proper construction began in 1859, and the canal was completed in 1869.

92
00:06:14.468 --> 00:06:18.270
It is 101 miles long and about 984 feet wide at its narrowest point.

93
00:06:22.876 --> 00:06:27.260
Over 1.5 million people were forced to work on the canal, mostly by hand.

94
00:06:28.281 --> 00:06:29.942
Would you like to dig a canal by hand?

95
00:06:30.803 --> 00:06:31.604
I certainly wouldn't!
